* It must not be confused with an ordinary Void elimination during an active match.

Keep the existing introductory and overtime announcements where appropriate.

The final announcement should take precedence over the old K.O. and terminal TIME banners.

Remove the old K.O. end-of-match announcement and references to it from the current results presentation. Do not remove unrelated legitimate combat terminology or gameplay mechanics.

4. New CPU Victory Celebration System

This is one of the most important features.

When a CPU-controlled fighter wins a match, it must no longer become completely inactive simply because the competitive match ended.

Instead, the winning CPU can enter a dedicated celebration behavior mode.

This applies to:

* The CPU winning Quick Battle against Player 1.
* CPU I winning Watch Mode.
* CPU II winning Watch Mode.

The celebration system must be separate from normal combat intelligence so that it does not interfere with ordinary CPU matches or difficulty balancing.

Five possible celebration behaviors

A winning CPU should randomly select from these five actions.

Behavior A — Random Ability Spam

The CPU repeatedly attempts to perform randomly selected abilities from its actual fighter moveset.

Requirements:

* Use the fighter’s existing available attacks and abilities.
* Support grounded and airborne moves where appropriate.
* Respect genuine ability availability, cooldowns, Energy costs, and animation locks.
* Randomize ability selection and input timing to create a visibly chaotic celebration.
* Do not hardcode fighter-specific abilities.
* Work with every playable fighter, including future additions.
* Do not bypass combat rules or force invalid attacks to activate.

The behavior should feel like a fighter celebrating through repeated special moves and attacks.

Behavior B — Run Into the Void

The CPU may deliberately decide to leave the stage and fall into the Void.

Requirements:

* Choose an appropriate direction toward a reachable Void boundary.
* Run or otherwise use normal movement to travel toward it.
* Do not prevent this action through the normal CPU’s edge-avoidance safety logic.
* Do not teleport the CPU out of the arena.
* Allow the actual stage physics and Void collision system to eliminate it.
* Play the normal Void elimination visual effects.
* Never respawn the fighter afterward.

This behavior must also work if overtime has already changed the Void boundaries.

The intentional Void run must be exclusive to the post-match state. Never weaken normal combat AI’s survival behavior.

Behavior C — Stay Idle

The CPU may choose to stand still.

Requirements:

* Remain in the normal idle animation.
* Avoid intentionally moving or performing attacks.
* Allow the game world and stage animations to continue.
* Remain idle for a meaningful interval rather than one frame.
* If the CPU later changes celebration behavior, transition naturally.

Behavior D — Dash Dance

The CPU rapidly moves left and right within a small area, imitating a player repeatedly changing direction.

Requirements:

* Alternate left and right inputs.
* Keep the CPU relatively close to one location.
* Use actual movement animations and existing movement physics.
* Create a fast, playful back-and-forth movement effect.
* Prevent the behavior from accidentally walking off the stage unless the CPU has separately selected the Void-running behavior.
* Do not create artificial movement values or animation shortcuts.

The action should resemble rapid left-right movement or dash dancing, not ordinary long-distance patrol movement.

Behavior E — Jump Spam

The CPU repeatedly jumps.

Requirements:

* Use actual fighter jump input.
* Repeatedly initiate jumps whenever legally possible.
* Allow normal air jumps where the fighter supports them.
* Respect the game’s jumping and landing mechanics.
* Release and repress Jump as needed so each jump is a valid input.
* Avoid permanently holding Jump without producing subsequent jumps.

Celebration behavior selection

Select behaviors randomly from the five available actions.

Use sensible randomized action durations, except for the irreversible Void run.

A CPU may move from one behavior to another while celebrating.

For example:

* Idle.
* Dash dance.
* Spam abilities.
* Jump repeatedly.
* Run into the Void.

The CPU must not be forced to do all five behaviors every time.

Each match should produce varied celebrations.

Use a dedicated, seedable random stream or a similarly reproducible mechanism. Avoid disturbing normal combat AI randomness or seeded match reproducibility.

Once a CPU has fallen into the Void during celebration, it remains eliminated and must not choose further actions.

Watch Mode behavior

When a Watch Mode match has a winner, the winning CPU uses this celebration system.

A surviving losing CPU may remain neutral or continue noncompetitive movement, but it must not trigger a new match or alter the finalized outcome.

For a draw, allow surviving CPUs to remain animated and optionally perform harmless post-match actions. No CPU should be labeled the winner.

Human player behavior

In Quick Battle, the human player must retain gameplay control after the match ends, provided their fighter remains in the arena.

The player should still be able to:

* Run.
* Jump.
* Dash.
* Perform attacks.
* Use special abilities.
* Move off the stage.

Do not automatically disable gameplay input merely because the match has ended.

The player may continue interacting with the arena or simply watch the celebration.

5. No Respawning During Celebration

After the match is officially over, disable all fighter respawning.

This applies equally to human players and CPUs.

Expected behavior

If a CPU runs into the Void during celebration:

* The CPU disappears using the normal elimination effects.
* It does not respawn.

If the human player jumps into the Void during celebration:

* The player’s fighter disappears using the normal elimination effects.
* The player does not respawn.

If both fighters fall into the Void:

* Both remain eliminated.
* The arena and applicable stage animations continue.
* The final result toast remains visible.
* The player can still open the Match Results dialog and use its navigation buttons.

Important edge cases

* A fighter eliminated by the final winning point must not respawn.
* A fighter already waiting to respawn when a match ends due to time or overtime must not return afterward.
* Post-match Void eliminations must not award points.
* Post-match eliminations must not modify the winner.
* A post-match fall must never restart overtime or trigger another end announcement.

During an active match, preserve existing Void scoring and two-second respawn behavior exactly as before.

