7. Full Match Results Dialog

Clicking View match results opens a comprehensive Match Results dialog.

Unlike the existing result overlay, this dialog must not pause the game simulation.

Core behavior

* Display the finalized outcome.
* Show both teams and their selected fighters.
* Show the winning and losing teams.
* Show detailed match statistics.
* Include the four required navigation buttons.
* Include a close button at the upper-right.
* Continue updating and rendering the battle behind the dialog.
* Do not change the frozen score or official match outcome.

The dialog can use modal focus management to make its buttons accessible, but its visibility must not be used as a reason to stop the simulation.

Dialog header

At the top, display:

MATCH RESULTS

Below it, show the result:

* TEAM I — WINNER / TEAM II — DEFEATED
* TEAM II — WINNER / TEAM I — DEFEATED
* Or DRAW — NO WINNER

Also show both fighters’ names and portraits.

Use consistent formatting and colors for each team.

For draws, treat both teams symmetrically. Do not display a defeated team when neither side won.

Statistics section

Create a detailed comparison of both teams’ performance throughout the match.

At minimum, include:

Statistic	Team I	Team II
Points	Value	Value
Total Damage Dealt	Value	Value
Total Damage Received	Value	Value
Total Launch Point Recovered	Value	Value
Successful Blocks	Value	Value
Successful Deflects	Value	Value
Total Energy Consumed	Value	Value
Void Falls	Value	Value
Successful Attacks	Value	Value

Additional meaningful statistics may include:

* Total attacks attempted.
* Total attacks landed.
* Perfect Shields.
* Damage blocked.
* Highest single hit damage.
* Ability activations.
* Ground and air dashes.
* Final Launch Point at match end.
* Final Energy at match end.
* Match duration.
* Overtime participation.
* Match ending reason.

Only display additional metrics that can be measured reliably.

Do not invent statistics or estimate them from the current fighter state.

Statistical definitions

Total Damage Dealt

The total actual damage successfully inflicted on an opponent during the competitive match.

Blocked hits do not count as damage dealt.

Projectile, summon, technique, Deflect attacks, and melee damage must credit the correct owning fighter.

Total Damage Received

The actual damage received during the match.

Count relevant hazard damage in the receiving fighter’s total where applicable, but do not incorrectly credit environmental damage to the opponent.

Total Launch Point Recovered

The cumulative Launch Point removed through legitimate recovery or healing during the match.

Include passive Launch Point recovery and actual healing effects.

Do not count Launch Point reset to zero during respawn or match restart as recovery.

Successful Blocks

Count actual incoming hits stopped by an active Shield.

Do not count the duration spent shielding.

A Perfect Shield is also a successful block; it may additionally count toward a separate Perfect Shield statistic.

Successful Deflects

Count successful projectile redirections caused by the fighter’s Deflect.

Do not count an attempted Deflect that redirected nothing.

Total Energy Consumed

The sum of actual Energy spent on valid actions throughout the match.

Include:

* Dash.
* Air Dash.
* Dash cancels.
* Deflects.
* Shield blocks.
* Construct-producing abilities.
* Other legitimate Energy-consuming actions.

Measure the actual Energy deducted, including partial payments when the fighter has less Energy than the nominal cost.

Do not confuse Energy consumed with current Energy, passive regeneration, or the Energy restored on respawn.

Points

The actual final competitive score.

Never include post-match Void falls.

Statistics tracking architecture

The current code exposes useful combat events, but a complete match-long statistics system should be implemented if one does not already exist.

Create a clean, dedicated match-stat tracking component or equivalent architecture.

Requirements:

* Track statistics separately for each side (p1 and p2 internally).
* Record cumulative values across all lives and respawns.
* Initialize on a new match.
* Reset fully on Rematch or Restart Battle.
* Increment counters from actual gameplay events.
* Avoid counting the same hit or action more than once.
* Attribute projectile, summon, and technique events correctly.
* Record actual Energy spending at its source.
* Measure Launch Point recovery from actual reductions.
* Finalize an immutable statistics snapshot at the exact end of the competitive match.
* Never modify finalized statistics during celebration.

Prefer observing existing combat and resource events rather than duplicating combat logic.

Keep the tracking system independent of which fighters were selected.

Results presentation

Design the dialog as a responsive comparison panel:

* Team I and Team II appear in clearly separated columns on desktop.
* The team that won is visually emphasized.
* Each statistic has a clear label.
* Values align neatly for direct comparison.
* Numeric formatting is consistent.
* On smaller screens, the statistics remain readable.
* Long content scrolls within the dialog without forcing the whole application to scroll.

Use the existing Alva dark/glass UI. Do not introduce an unrelated design language.

